      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate markdown carefully.\n\n**Certificate details:**\n- Certificate No: R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 Rev. 3\n- Scheme: A\n- Member State: SLOVAKIA\n- Issuing Authority: Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\n- Date issued: 28 January 2025\n- Pages: 13\n- Recommendation: OIML R 49, Edition 2013\n- Accuracy class: 2\n- Project number: FK-P05-85? That appears on each page footer. Also \"V04/24-12-02\" — form version. The project number could be \"2025/ER006/SK1\" — no, that's the evaluation report. Let me check: \"FK-P05-85\" appears to be a form code. The evaluation report No. 2025/ER006/SK1. Hmm, project_number — I'll use \"FK-P05-85\" or maybe omit. Actually project_number likely refers to the OIML CS project number. The certificate number contains \"23.02\". Hmm. I think \"FK-P05-85\" is a form identifier. I'll leave project_number as null or omit. Actually, revision history mentions \"2022/ER010/SK1_ Rev. 1\" as an updated evaluation report — conflicting with 2025/ER006/SK1. The evaluation report on page 2 is \"No. 2025/ER006/SK1 dated 28 January 2025 that includes 17 pages.\"\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\n- Address: Geologická 9966/1, 821 06 Bratislava-Podunajské Biskupice, Slovakia; Product Certification Body, Hviezdoslavova 31, 974 01 Banská Bystrica, Slovakia\n- Person responsible: Ing. Dušan Šmigura, PhD., Director of PCB\n\n**Applicant:**\n- UAB „Axioma Metering\", Veterinaru str. 52, Biruliskes, LT-54469 Kaunas region, Lithuania\n\n**Manufacturer:**\n- Same as applicant.\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: Water meter (category options given include \"Gas meter\", etc.; water meter fits). Category: \"Water meter\"\n- Type designations: \"Qalcosonic W1\" / \"QALCOSONIC W1\"\n- Module designation: \"Ultrasonic water meters with electronic indication device\"\n- Description: from section 1.\n\n**Model family:** The cert covers DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32, DN40, with various Q3 values and multiple variants each. This is a big matrix.\n\nLet me parse the tables:\n\n**Tab. 1 — DN15:**\n- Nominal diameter DN: 15 mm\n- Permanent flowrate Q3: 1.6 m³/h (5 variants) and 2.5 m³/h (5 variants)\n- Q1: 0.020, 0.010, 0.0064, 0.005, 0.004 (for Q3=1.6); 0.031, 0.0156, 0.010, 0.0062, 0.0031 (for Q3=2.5)\n- Q2: 0.032, 0.016, 0.010, 0.008, 0.0064; 0.050, 0.025, 0.016, 0.010, 0.005\n- Q4: 2 (for Q3=1.6); 3.125 (for Q3=2.5)\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: 80, 160, 250, 315, 400; 80, 160, 250, 400, 800\n\nWait, check: for Q3=2.5, ratios listed: 80, 160, 250, 400, 800. Q1 = Q3/ratio: 2.5/80 = 0.03125 ≈ 0.031 ✓; 2.5/160 = 0.015625 ≈ 0.0156 ✓; 2.5/250 = 0.010 ✓; 2.5/400 = 0.00625 ≈ 0.0062 ✓; 2.5/800 = 0.003125 ≈ 0.0031 ✓. Good.\n\nFor Q3=1.6: 1.6/80 = 0.020 ✓; 1.6/160 = 0.010 ✓; 1.6/250 = 0.0064 ✓; 1.6/315 = 0.00508 ≈ 0.005 ✓; 1.6/400 = 0.004 ✓.\n\n- Ratio Q2/Q1: 1.6 (all)\n- Connection thread: G ¾\n- Construction length L: 80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170 mm\n- Installation position: Horizontal / Vertical\n- Water temperature range: 0.1 to 30; 0.1 to 50; 30 to 70; 0.1 to 70 °C\n- Meter temperature class: T30, T50, T30/70, T70\n- Maximum working pressure MAP: 16 bar\n- Pressure loss class ΔP: 0.16 bar (Q3=1.6); 0.25 bar (Q3=2.5)\n- MPE upper: ±2 (at T ≤ 30°C), ±3 (at T > 30°C) — note \"Ø\" is OCR artifact for temperature symbol θ probably\n- MPE lower: ±5 %\n- Scale interval: 0.001 m³\n- Scale interval in testing mode: 0.000001 m³\n- Capacity of calculator: 999999.999 m³\n- Capacity of calculator in testing mode: 999.999999 m³\n- Accuracy class: 2\n- Mechanical class: M1\n- Climatic class: -15 to +70 °C\n- Electromagnetic class: E2\n- Environmental classification: B/O\n- Flow profile sensitivity class: U0D0\n- Battery: li-battery 3.6 V, life time 16 years\n\n**Tab. 2 — DN20:**\n- Q3: 2.5 (4 variants), 4 (5 variants)\n- Q1: 0.031, 0.0156, 0.010, 0.0062; 0.050, 0.025, 0.016, 0.010, 0.005\n- Q2: 0.05, 0.025, 0.016, 0.010; 0.080, 0.040, 0.026, 0.016, 0.008\n- Q4: 3.125; 5\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: 80, 160, 250, 400; 80, 160, 250, 400, 800\n- Connection thread: G 1\n- Construction length: 105, 110, 130, 165, 190 mm\n- Pressure loss: 0.16 (Q3=2.5); 0.25 (Q3=4)\n- rest same.\n\nCheck Q3=2.5: 2.5/80=0.031 ✓, 2.5/160=0.0156 ✓, 2.5/250=0.010 ✓, 2.5/400=0.0062 ✓. Q3=4: 4/80=0.05 ✓, 4/160=0.025 ✓, 4/250=0.016 ✓, 4/400=0.010 ✓, 4/800=0.005 ✓.\n\n**Tab. 3 — DN25:**\n- Q3: 6.3 (4 variants), 10 (4 variants)\n- Q1: 0.079, 0.040, 0.252 (likely OCR error — should be 0.0252), 0.016; 0.125, 0.0625, 0.040, 0.025\n- Q2: 0.126, 0.063, 0.040, 0.026; 0.200, 0.100, 0.064, 0.040\n- Q4: 7.875; 12.5\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: 80, 160, 250, 400; 80, 160, 250, 400\n\nCheck Q3=6.3: 6.3/80 = 0.07875 ≈ 0.079 ✓; 6.3/160 = 0.0394 ≈ 0.040 ✓; 6.3/250 = 0.0252 (the OCR shows 0.252 — likely OCR error, actual 0.0252) ✓; 6.3/400 = 0.01575 ≈ 0.016 ✓. Q3=10: 10/80=0.125 ✓; 10/160=0.0625 ✓; 10/250=0.040 ✓; 10/400=0.025 ✓.\n\nShould I correct 0.252 → 0.0252? The rule \"don't fabricate\" — but here the value 0.252 is clearly inconsistent (Q1 must be less than Q2 = 0.040). Given the pattern, 0.0252 is correct. I'll record 0.0252 since it matches the ratio 250 pattern and Q2=0.040. Hmm, risky but reasonable. Actually, I should be careful about \"don't fabricate\". The value 0.252 contradicts the table itself (Q1=0.252 > Q2=0.040 and ratio says 250 → 6.3/250=0.0252). I'll use 0.0252 and maybe not mention. Alternatively record as-is 0.252. I think correcting is justified given internal consistency. Let me use 0.0252.\n\n- Connection thread: G 1 ¼\n- Construction length: 260 mm\n- Pressure loss: 0.25 (Q3=6.3); 0.63 (Q3=10)\n\n**Tab. 4 — DN32:**\n- Q3: 6.3 (4 variants), 10 (3 variants)\n- Q1: 0.079, 0.040, 0.0252, 0.016; 0.125, 0.0625, 0.025\n- Q2: 0.126, 0.063, 0.040, 0.026; 0.200, 0.100, 0.040\n- Q4: 7.875; 12.5\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: 80, 160, 250, 400; 80, 160, 400\n\nCheck Q3=10, ratio 400: 10/400 = 0.025 ✓. Q2 for that: 0.025*1.6 = 0.040 ✓.\n\n- Connection thread: G 1 ½\n- Construction length: 260\n- Pressure loss: 0.16 (Q3=6.3); 0.25 (Q3=10)\n\n**Tab. 5 — DN40:**\n- Q3: 10 (3 variants), 16 (4 variants), 25 (4 variants)\n- Q1: 0.125, 0.0625, 0.040; 0.200, 0.100, 0.064, 0.040; 0.3125, 0.156, 0.100, 0.0625\n\nWait, 0.156 should probably be 0.15625? For Q3=25, ratio 160: 25/160 = 0.15625. The table says 0.156. Keep 0.156 as given. Actually wait — check ratio list for Q3=25: 80, 160, 250, 400. 25/80 = 0.3125 ✓; 25/160 = 0.15625 ≈ 0.156 (table) ✓; 25/250 = 0.100 ✓; 25/400 = 0.0625 ✓.\n\nFor Q3=16: ratios 80, 160, 250, 400: 16/80 = 0.200 ✓; 16/160 = 0.100 ✓; 16/250 = 0.064 ✓; 16/400 = 0.040 ✓.\n\nFor Q3=10: ratios 80, 160, 250: 0.125, 0.0625, 0.040 ✓.\n\n- Q2: 0.200, 0.100, 0.064; 0.320, 0.160, 0.102, 0.064; 0.500, 0.250, 0.160, 0.100\n\nCheck: Q2 = 1.6*Q1: 0.125*1.6 = 0.200 ✓; 0.0625*1.6 = 0.100 ✓; 0.040*1.6 = 0.064 ✓; 0.200*1.6 = 0.320 ✓; 0.100*1.6=0.160 ✓; 0.064*1.6=0.1024 ≈ 0.102 ✓; 0.040*1.6=0.064 ✓; 0.3125*1.6=0.500 ✓; 0.156*1.6=0.2496≈0.250 ✓; 0.100*1.6=0.160 ✓; 0.0625*1.6=0.100 ✓.\n\n- Q4: 12.5; 20.0; 31.25\n- Connection thread: G 2\n- Construction length: 300 mm\n- Pressure loss: 0.16 bar (all)\n- rest same.\n\nSo total variants: DN15: 10, DN20: 9, DN25: 8, DN32: 7, DN40: 11. Total 45 variants.\n\nHow to model? Options:\n- model_family with models keyed like \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R80\" etc.\n- matrix_tables preserving Tab. 1–5.\n- model_level characteristics: dn, q3, q1, q2, q4, ratio_q3_q1, pressure_loss...\n\nI think the cleanest: create models for each (DN, Q3, ratio) combination. That's 45 models. That may be verbose but faithful. Alternatively, models per DN (5 models) and put variation in model_level with composite model ids.\n\nLet me create model IDs like \"W1-DN15-Q3-1.6-R80\". Hmm, but the cert doesn't give explicit model designations beyond QALCOSONIC W1 with DN. I'll use model_id like \"DN15_Q3=1.6_Q3/Q1=80\".\n\nLet me enumerate:\n\nDN15 (Q3=1.6): R80 (Q1=0.020, Q2=0.032, Q4=2), R160 (0.010, 0.016, 2), R250 (0.0064, 0.010, 2), R315 (0.005, 0.008, 2), R400 (0.004, 0.0064, 2)\nDN15 (Q3=2.5): R80 (0.031, 0.050, 3.125), R160 (0.0156, 0.025, 3.125), R250 (0.010, 0.016, 3.125), R400 (0.0062, 0.010, 3.125), R800 (0.0031, 0.005, 3.125)\nDN20 (Q3=2.5): R80 (0.031, 0.05, 3.125), R160 (0.0156, 0.025, 3.125), R250 (0.010, 0.016, 3.125), R400 (0.0062, 0.010, 3.125)\nDN20 (Q3=4): R80 (0.050, 0.080, 5), R160 (0.025, 0.040, 5), R250 (0.016, 0.026, 5), R400 (0.010, 0.016, 5), R800 (0.005, 0.008, 5)\nDN25 (Q3=6.3): R80 (0.079, 0.126, 7.875), R160 (0.040, 0.063, 7.875), R250 (0.0252, 0.040, 7.875), R400 (0.016, 0.026, 7.875)\nDN25 (Q3=10): R80 (0.125, 0.200, 12.5), R160 (0.0625, 0.100, 12.5), R250 (0.040, 0.064, 12.5), R400 (0.025, 0.040, 12.5)\nDN32 (Q3=6.3): R80 (0.079, 0.126, 7.875), R160 (0.040, 0.063, 7.875), R250 (0.0252, 0.040, 7.875), R400 (0.016, 0.026, 7.875)\nDN32 (Q3=10): R80 (0.125, 0.200, 12.5), R160 (0.0625, 0.100, 12.5), R400 (0.025, 0.040, 12.5)\nDN40 (Q3=10): R80 (0.125, 0.200, 12.5), R160 (0.0625, 0.100, 12.5), R250 (0.040, 0.064, 12.5)\nDN40 (Q3=16): R80 (0.200, 0.320, 20.0), R160 (0.100, 0.160, 20.0), R250 (0.064, 0.102, 20.0), R400 (0.040, 0.064, 20.0)\nDN40 (Q3=25): R80 (0.3125, 0.500, 31.25), R160 (0.156, 0.250, 31.25), R250 (0.100, 0.160, 31.25), R400 (0.0625, 0.100, 31.25)\n\nThat's 10+9+8+7+11 = 45 models.\n\nFor model_level characteristics, I could emit per-attribute lists with 45 values each for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, dn... That's a lot but doable. Alternatively, put DN as attribute of each model. Let me use model_level entries for: nominal_diameter, permanent_flowrate_q3, minimum_flowrate_q1, transitional_flowrate_q2, overload_flowrate_q4, ratio_q3_q1, pressure_loss_delta_p. And connection_thread and construction_length vary by DN — I could emit model_level with values per DN-group model, or use config_level. Since models are DN+Q3+ratio combos, connection thread and construction length are constant per DN. I could emit model_level entries listing them per model (45 entries each) — verbose. Alternatively emit them in matrix_tables rows and type_level not possible.\n\nHmm. Let me think about the balance. The schema says matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables; model_level is attribute × model matrix. I'll do:\n\n- matrix_tables: 5 tables (Tab 1–5), with columns and rows per variant. This preserves everything faithfully.\n- model_family.models: 45 entries with model_id like \"DN15_R80_Q3=1.6\" and attributes including dn, q3, ratio, connection_thread, construction_length, pressure_loss. Actually attributes{} per model could carry connection_thread etc. That reduces repetition in model_level.\n\nPlan: model_family.models[] each with model_id and attributes {nominal_diameter, q3, q1, q2, q4, ratio_q3_q1, connection_thread, construction_length, pressure_loss_delta_p}. Then characteristics.model_level for the flow-rate attributes (q3, q1, q2, q4, ratio, dn, pressure_loss) — but that duplicates. Maybe model_level for a few key attributes and rely on models[].attributes for the rest? The schema shows models[] with attributes{} — so I can put all varying attributes there and use model_level for a subset. But the R137 example populates both matrix_tables and model_level. To be safe and consistent, I'll populate model_level for the main flow attributes (q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, pressure_loss_delta_p, nominal_diameter) with 45 values each... that's 7 × 45 = 315 value objects. Very verbose but the instruction emphasizes matrix extraction. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, use model_id = DN-based (5 models: DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32, DN40) and treat Q3 and ratio as config axes via config_level? The variation within DN is \"metrological variant\" — could be config axis \"ratio_q3_q1\". Config-level: attribute q1, axis \"dn + q3 + ratio\"? That gets awkward.\n\nI think per-variant models is cleanest and most faithful. Let me reduce verbosity: model_level entries for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, pressure_loss, dn. Values arrays of 45. And models[] entries with just model_id (45 entries). And matrix_tables with the full rows including construction lengths, threads, etc.\n\nActually the R137 example does exactly this: matrix_tables with full rows AND model_level with the key attributes. I'll follow that pattern. models[] just model_id.\n\nType-level characteristics (same across all): ratio_q2_q1 (1.6), installation_position (Horizontal/Vertical), water_temperature_range (multiple classes), meter_temperature_class (T30/T50/T30/70/T70), max_working_pressure_map (16 bar), mpe_upper, mpe_lower, scale_interval (0.001 m³), scale_interval_testing (0.000001 m³), calculator_capacity (999999.999 m³), calculator_capacity_testing (999.999999 m³), accuracy_class (2), mechanical_class (M1), climatic_class (-15 to +70 °C), electromagnetic_class (E2), environmental_classification (B/O), flow_profile_sensitivity_class (U0D0), battery (li-battery 3.6 V, 16 years).\n\nBattery: 3.6 V — could split: battery_voltage 3.6 V; battery_life 16 years; battery_type \"lithium\".\n\nWater temperature range: four ranges tied to temperature classes: T30: 0.1–30; T50: 0.1–50; T30/70: 30–70; T70: 0.1–70. Use config_level with axis \"meter_temperature_class\": conditions T30, T50, T30/70, T70, values ranges in °C. That's a good use of config_level.\n\nMPE upper: ±2% at T ≤ 30°C, ±3% at T > 30°C — config_level axis \"water_temperature\". Value \"±2\" — signed. Value could be string \"±2\" or number? MPE is ±2%; store as {\"value\": -2, ...}? Better keep as string \"±2\"? Hmm. The rules say numbers not strings, but ±2% is a tolerance. I'll use value \"±2\" ... Actually maybe value: 2 with attribute \"maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone\" and note. I'll keep it simple: value as string \"±2\"? The strict rule says numbers; but ±2% is symmetric tolerance. I'll use {\"value\": -2, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}? No — MPE ±2 means the error must be within ±2%, i.e., range -2 to +2. Could express as {min: -2, max: 2}. That's elegant: value {min:-2, max:2}, unit \"%\". For lower: {min:-5, max:5} %.\n\nMPE upper has condition (temp ≤ 30°C vs > 30°C) → config_level with axis \"water_temperature\".\n\nComponents: flow sensor + calculator/indication device. The cert describes essential parts: flow sensor, calculator and indication device. I'll add components[]:\n- role \"flow sensor (measuring transducer)\", type_designations [], characteristics: description...\n- role \"calculator and indication device\", characteristics: display info, battery.\n\nMaybe also integrated RF transceiver etc. as type_level attribute.\n\nSoftware: model_level? Software versions map to DN groups: 1.03/C983 for DN15–DN32 (design A), 1.03/F897 for DN15 B, DN20 B, 2.02/CD00 for DN40. This is a table — put in matrix_tables \"Software versions\" with rows. Columns: software_version, checksum, remarks (applicable models).\n\nModel IDs: since design A vs B matters for software, model ids could include design? Rev 3 adds design B for DN15 and DN20. Designs differ only in external appearance. I'll keep model ids without design but mention in attributes? Hmm, keep simpler: model_id per DN/Q3/ratio; design A default. Could add \"design\" attribute? Not clean. I'll leave as is and note designs in matrix table description or software table remarks.\n\nFootnotes: none present in this cert. So footnotes: [] or omit. No footnote markers. Omit.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013, scheme \"A\", accuracy_classes [2]. Wait schema: recommendation.id canonical e.g. \"R60\"; scheme under recommendation? The schema shows scheme under recommendation too. Certificate scheme \"A\".\n\ntest_reports[]:\n- id \"2025/ER006/SK1\", date \"2025-01-28\", pages 17, role \"type_evaluation_report\"\n- documentation files:\n  - \"AXIOMA_Qalcosonic W1_00\" dated 13 Jan 2023, 114 pages, role \"documentation_file\"\n  - \"AXIOMA_Qalcosonic W1_03\" dated 28 Jan 2025, 51 pages, role \"documentation_file\"\n- Revision 2 mentions \"2022/ER010/SK1_ Rev. 1\" (updated evaluation report) — include in revision_history changes only.\n\nDates: ISO format \"2025-01-28\" etc. The markdown has \"28st January 2025\" (OCR of 28th). Use 2025-01-28.\n\ndate_issued: 2025-01-28 (signature date).\n\ncertificate.page_total: 13. member_state: \"Slovakia\" (page says SLOVAKIA). oiml_issuer_id: not given explicitly? The certificate number includes \"SK1\" which is the issuer ID for SLM. Hmm, \"oiml_issuer_id\" — for NMi it's like \"NMi\" code. SK1 appears in report number \"2025/ER006/SK1\". I could set oiml_issuer_id: \"SK1\". That's plausible. I'll include it in certificate and issuing_authority.\n\nrevision_history: revisions \"0\"–\"3\" with dates and changes.\n\nrevision 0: 2023-01-13, \"Certificate first issued\"\nrevision 1: 2023-05-25, \"Point 2.4 Software specification – supplemented specification for all DNs separately\"\nrevision 2: 2024-05-31, \"Issued updated Evaluation Report No: 2022/ER010/SK1_ Rev. 1\"\nrevision 3: 2025-01-28, \"Add design B (DN15 and DN20); Changed address of the issuing authority SLM on the EU-type examination certificate\"\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\"\n- address_lines: [\"Geologická 9966/1\", \"821 06 Bratislava-Podunajské Biskupice, Slovakia\", \"Product Certification Body\", \"Hviezdoslavova 31\", \"974 01 Banská Bystrica, Slovakia\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Ing. Dušan Šmigura, PhD.\"\n- person_title: \"Director of PCB\"\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"Qalcosonic W1\"] (also \"QALCOSONIC W1\" — same, keep one; maybe both spellings? Use \"QALCOSONIC W1\"? Page 1 uses \"Qalcosonic W1\", section 1 uses \"QALCOSONIC W1\". I'll use \"Qalcosonic W1\" as in identification, and mention QALCOSONIC W1? Just one: \"Qalcosonic W1\". Hmm — tables use \"QALCOSONIC W1\". I'll include both? Type designations all type/model identifiers mentioned — I'd say [\"Qalcosonic W1\"] suffices; case-insensitive same type. I'll use \"QALCOSONIC W1\"? Let me just use \"Qalcosonic W1\" as it's the formal identification on the certificate face.\n- module_designation: \"Ultrasonic water meters with electronic indication device\"\n- description: from section 1: \"Compact ultrasonic water meter with electronic indication device...\" I'll write a concise description.\n\nmodel_family.family_name: \"QALCOSONIC W1\".\n\ncomponents:\n1. role: \"flow sensor\", characteristics: description text (brass body..., two ultrasonic transducers (four for DN40), inner plastic element with two mirrors (four for DN40)).\n2. role: \"calculator and indication device\", characteristics: display 2-line LCD; upper line 9 digits, range 999999.999 m³; lower line 5 digits flow rate m³/h; batteries non-replaceable lithium, life 16 years; battery configurations per DN.\n3. Integrated RF transceiver etc. — maybe type_level attribute \"integrated_communication\": \"Wireless M-Bus, LoRa, NFC, NB-IoT radio\" and mcu: \"Renesas RF10WMGAFB (128 KB) or Renesas R5F111PJGFB (256 KB)\".\n\nConfig-level: water_temperature_range per temperature class. Also MPE per temperature.\n\nLet me now build matrix_tables:\n\nTable \"Tab. 1 – Metrological data DN15\":\ncolumns: [\"nominal_diameter\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\", \"ratio_q2_q1\", \"connection_thread\", \"construction_length\", ...]? \n\nThe original table is column-per-attribute, rows = header rows (attribute, unit) then data columns... Actually the markdown table has attributes as rows and values as columns (10 variants per DN for Tab1). For matrix_tables rows-per-variant format, I'll pivot: each row = one variant.\n\nTo keep size manageable, in matrix_tables rows I'll include: dn, q3, q1, q2, q4, ratio_q3_q1, connection_thread, construction_length_options, pressure_loss_delta_p. Common attributes (ratio_q2_q1, installation, temperature, MPE, etc.) go in type_level.\n\nBut construction lengths are lists (\"80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170\") applying to the whole DN — not per variant. So per-variant rows: dn, q3, ratio_q3_q1, q1, q2, q4, pressure_loss_delta_p. Then DN-level attributes (connection thread, construction lengths) as description or as separate rows? I could add them to the table description: \"DN15: connection thread G 3/4; construction lengths 80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170 mm.\"\n\nAlternatively add column \"connection_thread\" and \"construction_length_options\" repeated per row. Repetition is fine in JSON. Hmm, I'll include connection_thread per row (it's constant per DN but that's OK), and construction_lengths as a list in the table description. Actually let me add \"construction_length_mm\" column with an array value? Rows are objects; values can be arrays. E.g. {\"construction_length_mm\": [80, 105, ...]}. That's acceptable. But maybe simpler: put DN-level info in description.\n\nI'll structure matrix_tables entries:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"Tab. 1 – Technical and metrological data, DN15\",\n  \"columns\": [\"nominal_diameter\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null],\n  \"rows\": [ {dn:15, q3:1.6, q1:0.020, q2:0.032, q4:2, ratio_q3_q1:80}, ... 10 rows ],\n  \"description\": \"Connection thread G 3/4; construction length 80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170 mm; pressure loss class ΔP 0.16 bar (Q3=1.6) / 0.25 bar (Q3=2.5).\",\n}\n\nHmm, but pressure loss varies by Q3 — better as a row-level column \"pressure_loss_delta_p\" with unit bar. Let me add columns: pressure_loss_delta_p (bar), connection_thread (null), construction_length_mm (array). I'll include pressure_loss per row and connection_thread per row, construction_length as array per row (same for all rows of that DN). That's faithful and self-contained. Slight repetition OK.\n\nActually let me reconsider column units: \"column_units\": matching schema example. For connection_thread unit null, construction_length unit \"mm\", pressure loss \"bar\".\n\nNow model_level characteristics. Attributes: nominal_diameter (mm), permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h), minimum_flowrate_q1, transitional_flowrate_q2, overload_flowrate_q4, ratio_q3_q1, pressure_loss_delta_p (bar). 45 values each.\n\nModel ids: \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R80\" style. Let me define model_id strings:\nDN15: \n- \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R80\"\n- \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R160\"\n- \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R250\"\n- \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R315\"\n- \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R400\"\n- \"DN15_Q3=2.5_R80\"\n- \"DN15_Q3=2.5_R160\"\n- \"DN15_Q3=2.5_R250\"\n- \"DN15_Q3=2.5_R400\"\n- \"DN15_Q3=2.5_R800\"\nDN20:\n- \"DN20_Q3=2.5_R80\", R160, R250, R400\n- \"DN20_Q3=4_R80\", R160, R250, R400, R800\nDN25:\n- \"DN25_Q3=6.3_R80\", R160, R250, R400\n- \"DN25_Q3=10_R80\", R160, R250, R400\nDN32:\n- \"DN32_Q3=6.3_R80\", R160, R250, R400\n- \"DN32_Q3=10_R80\", R160, R400\nDN40:\n- \"DN40_Q3=10_R80\", R160, R250\n- \"DN40_Q3=16_R80\", R160, R250, R400\n- \"DN40_Q3=25_R80\", R160, R250, R400\n\nCount: 10+9+8+7+11 = 45. \n\nWait DN32 Q3=10 has 3 variants: ratios 80, 160, 400 → Q1 0.125, 0.0625, 0.025; Q2 0.200, 0.100, 0.040. Yes.\n\nDN40 Q3=10: 3 variants (80,160,250). Q3=16: 4 variants. Q3=25: 4 variants. Total 11. ✓.\n\nNow, values for each attribute per model:\n\nnominal_diameter: DN15→15 (10), DN20→20 (9), DN25→25 (8), DN32→32 (7), DN40→40 (11). Unit mm.\n\nq3: per model.\nq1, q2, q4, ratio, pressure loss per model.\n\npressure_loss_delta_p: DN15: Q3=1.6→0.16, Q3=2.5→0.25; DN20: 2.5→0.16, 4→0.25; DN25: 6.3→0.25, 10→0.63; DN32: 6.3→0.16, 10→0.25; DN40: all→0.16. Unit bar.\n\nHmm wait DN25 pressure loss: \"0,25 | 0,63\" — Q3=6.3 group gets 0.25 and Q3=10 group gets 0.63. Yes. That seems odd (higher flow → higher pressure loss, makes sense).\n\nDN32: \"0,16 | 0,25\" — Q3=6.3→0.16, Q3=10→0.25. OK.\n\nconnection_thread: DN15: \"G 3/4\"; DN20: \"G 1\"; DN25: \"G 1 1/4\"; DN32: \"G 1 1/2\"; DN40: \"G 2\". These could be model_level too — but per-DN constant. I could emit connection_thread as model_level with 45 entries or fold into models[].attributes. To reduce bulk, I'll put connection_thread and construction lengths into matrix_tables description + models attributes? Let me put connection_thread in model_level as well — no, too repetitive. Decision: include connection_thread and construction_length_options in each model's attributes{} in model_family.models. Actually then models[] become heavy (45 × several attributes). Alternatively include per-DN mapping in a config_level? Config axis \"nominal_diameter\": attribute connection_thread, values [{condition \"DN15\", value \"G 3/4\"}, ...]. That's neat! config_level:\n\n- attribute \"connection_thread\", axis \"nominal_diameter\", values: [{condition \"DN15\", value \"G 3/4\"}, {\"DN20\", \"G 1\"}, {\"DN25\", \"G 1 1/4\"}, {\"DN32\", \"G 1 1/2\"}, {\"DN40\", \"G 2\"}]\n- attribute \"construction_length\", axis \"nominal_diameter\", values: [{condition \"DN15\", value [80,105,110,115,165,170], unit \"mm\"}, ...]\n\nHmm value arrays... The structured value format expects number/range/string. An array of options breaks the shape. I could store as string \"80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170\" with unit mm. That keeps shape. Or put construction lengths in matrix_tables descriptions only. I'll do config_level with string values for construction lengths. Actually cleaner: since construction length is an option list, value as string is acceptable.\n\nWater temperature ranges → config_level axis \"meter_temperature_class\":\n- attribute \"water_temperature_range\", axis \"meter_temperature_class\", values: [{condition \"T30\", value {min 0.1, max 30}, unit °C}, {\"T50\", {0.1,50}}, {\"T30/70\", {30,70}}, {\"T70\", {0.1,70}}]\n\nMPE upper → config_level axis \"water_temperature\":\n- attribute \"maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone\", axis \"water_temperature\", values: [{condition \"T ≤ 30°C\", value {min:-2,max:2}, unit \"%\"}, {condition \"T > 30°C\", value {min:-3,max:3}, unit \"%\"}]\n\nMPE lower → type_level: maximum_permitted_error_lower_zone {min:-5,max:5} %.\n\nType-level characteristics:\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6 (dimensionless)\n- installation_position: \"Horizontal / Vertical\"\n- meter_temperature_class: \"T30, T50, T30/70, T70\" (string) — value \"T30/T50/T30/70/T70\"? Keep string \"T30, T50, T30/70, T70\".\n- maximum_working_pressure_map: 16 bar\n- scale_interval: 0.001 m³\n- scale_interval_testing_mode: 0.000001 m³\n- calculator_capacity: 999999.999 m³\n- calculator_capacity_testing_mode: 999.999999 m³\n- accuracy_class: \"2\" or 2 — numeric 2? Class 2 — use number 2. Hmm classes are usually strings (\"2\"). value: 2 fine. I'll use 2 as number? For recommendation.accuracy_classes: [2]? Schema says accuracy_classes[] — for R60 they'd be like [\"C\"]. Use [2]? Or [\"2\"]? I'll use [2]? Hmm accuracy class 2 for water meters (R49 class 2). I'll use [2]... safer as string \"2\"? The schema example: accuracy_classes[] unspecified element type. I'll use [\"2\"]? Let me use [2] as number? For water meters class is denoted 2 or 3. I'll use [2]? I'll go with [2]? Hmm. Choose accuracy_classes: [2] as numbers? Actually strings safer for classes. Use [\"2\"]. Eh. I'll use [2]. Decision: [\"2\"]. No... \n\nThe R49 uses \"accuracy class 2\". Either is defensible. I'll use [2] number in recommendation and type_level accuracy_class value 2? Wait characteristic values should be numbers where numeric. Class \"2\" is a designation; number 2 is fine. I'll use 2.\n\nHmm, but mechanical class \"M1\", electromagnetic \"E2\", environmental \"B/O\", flow profile \"U0D0\" are strings.\n\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- climatic_class: {min:-15, max:70} °C\n- electromagnetic_class: \"E2\"\n- environmental_classification: \"B/O\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- battery_type: \"Lithium\" (li-battery)\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n- battery_life: 16 years (\"lifetime 16 years\")\n- battery_configurations: per DN — config_level? The battery configs: DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32: two AA; plus variants... The text is garbled (contradictory). I'll capture as string in components (calculator) characteristics or as a matrix in description. Maybe include in components[1].characteristics.battery_configuration as the raw bullet lines. Or skip detail. I'll add to matrix_tables? It's messy OCR. I'll include a \"battery_configuration\" string in the calculator component characteristics summarizing: \"DN15/DN20/DN25/DN32 (design A): 2 × AA; DN15/DN20 design B: 1 × C + 1 × A23; DN25/DN32: 2 × AA + 1 × A23; DN40: 1 × D (+ 1 × A23)\". Hmm the OCR lines conflict: \"for DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32 two internal batteries AA:size\"; \"for DN15 and DN15 B design, DN20 and DN20 B design, DN25, DN32: two AA + one A23\"; \"For DN15 B design, DN20 B design one internal battery C size + one A23\"; \"for DN40 one internal battery D size\"; \"for DN40 one internal battery D size + one A23\". I'll reproduce as-is-ish. Since it's ambiguous, I'll include as a string in component characteristics without over-interpretation.\n\n- display: upper line 9 digits, indication range 999999.999 m³; lower line 5 digits flow rate m³/h. Add to calculator component characteristics or type_level: display_indication_range 999999.999 m³; display_digits 9? I'll put in components.\n\n- integrated_communication: \"Wireless M-Bus, LoRa, NFC, NB-IoT\" type_level string.\n- mcu: \"Renesas RF10WMGAFB (128 KB) or Renesas R5F111PJGFB (256 KB)\" type_level string.\n\nSoftware table → matrix_tables entry:\n{\n name: \"Software versions\",\n columns: [\"software_version\", \"checksum\", \"applicable_models\"],\n column_units: [null, null, null],\n rows: [\n  {software_version: \"1.03\", checksum: \"C983\", applicable_models: \"DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32\"},\n  {software_version: \"1.03\", checksum: \"F897\", applicable_models: \"DN15 B design, DN20 B design\"},\n  {software_version: \"2.02\", checksum: \"CD00\", applicable_models: \"DN40\"}\n ]\n}\n\nSoftware version as string \"1.03\" — careful: JSON number 1.03 vs version string; keep string.\n\nFootnotes: none. Omit.\n\nNow certificate fields:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\" — with Rev. 3? The number is \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 Rev. 3\". I'll set number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\", revision: \"3\"? Schema has no revision field at certificate level, but revision_history exists. I'll put number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 Rev. 3\"? Hmm. Better: number \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02\" and include revision_history; but then the Rev. 3 designation is lost at the top. I'll include \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 Rev. 3\" as the number since that's how it's consistently printed. Let me use the full string including Rev. 3. Yes — every page header shows \"R49/2013-A-SK1-23.02 Rev. 3\".\n\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: not clearly present. FK-P05-85 is a form code; skip? The rules: don't fabricate. I'll omit project_number. Hmm, could set to \"FK-P05-85\"? It's a form template code (appears with page numbers and form version V04/24-12-02). Not a project number. Omit.\n- page_total: 13\n- member_state: \"Slovakia\"\n- date_issued: \"2025-01-28\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"SK1\"\n\nissuing_authority.oiml_issuer_id: \"SK1\".\n\nrecommendation: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, amendment: null?, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [2]}. Amendment not mentioned — omit or null. I'll omit amendment (rule 8: omit if not present). Actually \"Don't fabricate... omit it.\" So omit amendment. But scheme is given (\"ISSUED UNDER SCHEME A\"). Include scheme \"A\". accuracy_classes: [2]? I'll use [2]? Let me decide [2] numeric... Actually classes as strings is more typical in such schemas; but 2 is numeric. I'll use [2]. Hmm the example in schema says accuracy_classes[] with no type. Fine: [2].\n\nWait — recommendation scheme: the schema example includes scheme under recommendation. Certificate scheme A. Include both.\n\ntest_reports:\n1. {id: \"2025/ER006/SK1\", date: \"2025-01-28\", pages: 17, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n2. {id: \"AXIOMA_Qalcosonic W1_00\", date: \"2023-01-13\", pages: 114, role: \"documentation_file\"}\n3. {id: \"AXIOMA_Qalcosonic W1_03\", date: \"2025-01-28\", pages: 51, role: \"documentation_file\"}\n\nThe doc file names have a trailing „ in OCR (\"AXIOMA_Qalcosonic W1_00“\") — German-style closing quote artifact. Clean to \"AXIOMA_Qalcosonic W1_00\" and \"AXIOMA_Qalcosonic W1_03\".\n\nrevision_history as above.\n\nmodel_family:\n{\n  family_name: \"QALCOSONIC W1\",\n  models: [ {model_id: \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R80\", attributes: {design: \"A\"}}, ... ]\n}\n\nShould models have attributes? Include design: \"A\" for all, plus designs B apply to DN15/DN20 whole (design B same metrology, only appearance differs, but different software checksum). I could add attribute \"software_version\"/\"checksum\" per model? For DN15/DN20, design A → SW 1.03/C983; design B → SW 1.03/F897. So models could include design variants... 45 models × design? That'd be 55 (DN15 A+B, DN20 A+B). Getting complicated. Keep 45 metrological variants, and add attributes {designs: [\"A\"]} for DN15/DN20 and [\"A\"] for others... Hmm.\n\nSimpler: models without attributes, plus software matrix table conveying design B checksums. And a note in description. I'll keep models lean: just model_id. Maybe include attributes with design availability: DN15/DN20: designs A and B; others A only. I'll add \"attributes\": {\"designs\": [\"A\",\"B\"]} for DN15/DN20 models and {\"designs\": [\"A\"]} for the rest. That's informative and low-cost. Actually the R137 example has empty models entries {model_id}. I'll include designs attribute — helpful.\n\nNow, character-level details: value \"1,6\" → 1.6. Q1 \"0,020\" → 0.020 → JSON 0.02. Hmm — trailing zeros lost: 0.020 → 0.02. Fine numerically.\n\nDN25 Q1 R250: OCR \"0,252\" → correct to 0.0252? I reasoned yes. I'll do it (internal consistency: Q1 = Q3/ratio = 6.3/250 = 0.0252, and Q2 = 0.040 = 1.6 × 0.0252). Record 0.0252.\n\nDN40 Q1 R160 (Q3=25): \"0,156\" → 0.156 (as printed). Keep 0.156.\n\nDN20 Q2 for Q3=4 R250: printed \"0,026\" → 0.026 (actually 0.064*1.6=0.1024? wait). Let me recheck DN20 Q3=4: Q1 values 0.050, 0.025, 0.016, 0.010, 0.005; Q2 printed: 0.080, 0.040, 0.026, 0.016, 0.008. 1.6×0.016 = 0.0256 ≈ 0.026 ✓. OK keep.\n\nDN25 Q2 values: 0.126, 0.063, 0.040, 0.026 — 1.6×0.016=0.0256≈0.026 ✓. Keep.\n\nNow connection thread \"G ¾\" → \"G 3/4\". \"G 1 ¼\" → \"G 1 1/4\". \"G 1 ½\" → \"G 1 1/2\". Convert Unicode fractions to ASCII.\n\nTemperature ranges: \"0,1 to 30\" → {min: 0.1, max: 30}.\n\nClimatic class: -15 to +70 → {min:-15, max:70} °C.\n\nMPE upper: \"± 2 (at Ø ≤ 30°C)\" — Ø is OCR garbage for θ (temperature). Condition strings: \"water temperature ≤ 30 °C\" and \"> 30 °C\".\n\nBattery: \"li-battery 3,6 V, life time 16 years\" → battery_chemistry \"lithium\", battery_voltage 3.6 V, battery_life 16 years? I'll add type_level: battery_voltage {3.6, \"V\"}, battery_lifetime_years {16, \"a\"}? Unit for years: \"years\"? unit_symbol \"a\" (annum)? Use \"years\" as raw symbol. I'll use unit_symbol \"years\". Fine.\n\nNow components:\n\ncomponents[0]:\n{\n \"role\": \"flow sensor (measurement transducer)\",\n \"type_designations\": [],\n \"characteristics\": {\n   \"type_level\": {...}? \n }\n}\nSchema: components[]: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. characteristics{} free-form; I'll put keys directly like {\"description\": \"...\", \"ultrasonic_transducers\": \"2 (4 for DN40)\", \"sound_paths\": \"2 mirrors (4 mirrors for DN40)\", \"body\": \"plastic cylindrical brass body with plastic calculator housing\"}.\n\ncomponents[1]:\n{\n \"role\": \"calculator and indication device\",\n \"type_designations\": [],\n \"characteristics\": {\n   \"display\": \"2-line LCD; upper line 9 digits, indication range 999999.999 m³; lower line 5 digits for flow rate in m³/h\",\n   \"batteries\": \"non-replaceable lithium batteries, life 16 years\",\n   \"battery_configuration\": \"DN15/DN20/DN25/DN32: 2 × AA (design A); DN15/DN20 design B: 2 × AA + 1 × A23 or 1 × C + 1 × A23; DN40: 1 × D (+ 1 × A23)\",\n   \"mcu\": \"Renesas RF10WMGAFB (128 KB) or Renesas R5F111PJGFB (256 KB)\"\n }\n}\n\nHmm MCU is integrated equipment (point 2.6). Put in type_level instead. Fine either way; I'll put integrated_communication and mcu in type_level.\n\nNon-essential parts: antenna, optional non-return valve — mention in certified_type.description or components? Skip or put in description.\n\ncertified_type.description: \"Compact ultrasonic water meter with electronic indication device measuring volume of clean water for residential use; ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time measurement principle; produced in design A (all DN) and design B (DN15 and DN20 only, differing solely in external appearance); installation horizontal and vertical; not designed to measure reverse flow. Non-essential parts: antenna; optional non-return valve.\"\n\nconfig_level entries:\n\n1. water_temperature_range by meter_temperature_class (4 conditions)\n2. maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone by water_temperature (2 conditions)\n3. connection_thread by nominal_diameter (5)\n4. construction_length by nominal_diameter (5, string values)\n\nmodel_level entries (7 attributes × 45 values):\n- nominal_diameter (mm)\n- permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h)\n- minimum_flowrate_q1 (m³/h)\n- transitional_flowrate_q2 (m³/h)\n- overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h)\n- ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless)\n- pressure_loss_delta_p (bar)\n\nValues structure: {\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": N, \"unit_symbol\": ...}? The example in schema: {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} with unit at attribute level: {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [...]}. So unit_symbol per attribute entry. And footnote_markers optional. For values, {\"model\", \"value\"}; the structured-value shape {value, unit_symbol, unit_id} applies loosely; I'll follow the example: attribute-level unit_symbol, value plain numbers in values[].\n\nHmm but rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value/unit separation. The example for model_level shows values as {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — unit at attribute level. I'll follow the example (attribute-level unit_symbol) since it's the provided pattern.\n\nFor config_level: {condition, value, unit_symbol} per schema note.\n\nmatrix_tables: 6 tables (Tab1–Tab5 + software). Include footnotes: [] omit.\n\nLet me now write out all rows.\n\nTab 1 rows (DN15):\n1. {dn:15, q3:1.6, q1:0.020, q2:0.032, q4:2, ratio_q3_q1:80, pressure_loss:0.16, thread \"G 3/4\", lengths [80,105,110,115,165,170]}\n2. q1:0.010, q2:0.016, R160\n3. q1:0.0064, q2:0.010, R250\n4. q1:0.005, q2:0.008, R315\n5. q1:0.004, q2:0.0064, R400\n6. q3:2.5, q1:0.031, q2:0.050, q4:3.125, R80, Δp 0.25\n7. q1:0.0156, q2:0.025, R160\n8. q1:0.010, q2:0.016, R250\n9. q1:0.0062, q2:0.010, R400\n10. q1:0.0031, q2:0.005, R800\n\nTab 2 (DN20), thread \"G 1\", lengths [105,110,130,165,190]:\n1. q3:2.5, q1:0.031, q2:0.05, q4:3.125, R80, Δp 0.16\n2. q1:0.0156, q2:0.025, R160\n3. q1:0.010, q2:0.016, R250\n4. q1:0.0062, q2:0.010, R400\n5. q3:4, q1:0.050, q2:0.080, q4:5, R80, Δp 0.25\n6. q1:0.025, q2:0.040, R160\n7. q1:0.016, q2:0.026, R250\n8. q1:0.010, q2:0.016, R400\n9. q1:0.005, q2:0.008, R800\n\nTab 3 (DN25), thread \"G 1 1/4\", lengths [260]:\n1. q3:6.3, q1:0.079, q2:0.126, q4:7.875, R80, Δp 0.25\n2. q1:0.040, q2:0.063, R160\n3. q1:0.0252, q2:0.040, R250\n4. q1:0.016, q2:0.026, R400\n5. q3:10, q1:0.125, q2:0.200, q4:12.5, R80, Δp 0.63\n6. q1:0.0625, q2:0.100, R160\n7. q1:0.040, q2:0.064, R250\n8. q1:0.025, q2:0.040, R400\n\nTab 4 (DN32), thread \"G 1 1/2\", lengths [260]:\n1. q3:6.3, q1:0.079, q2:0.126, q4:7.875, R80, Δp 0.16\n2. q1:0.040, q2:0.063, R160\n3. q1:0.0252, q2:0.040, R250\n4. q1:0.016, q2:0.026, R400\n5. q3:10, q1:0.125, q2:0.200, q4:12.5, R80, Δp 0.25\n6. q1:0.0625, q2:0.100, R160\n7. q1:0.025, q2:0.040, R400\n\nTab 5 (DN40), thread \"G 2\", lengths [300]:\n1. q3:10, q1:0.125, q2:0.200, q4:12.5, R80, Δp 0.16\n2. q1:0.0625, q2:0.100, R160\n3. q1:0.040, q2:0.064, R250\n4. q3:16, q1:0.200, q2:0.320, q4:20.0, R80, Δp 0.16\n5. q