N15 2.5: 0.050, 0.025, 0.016, 0.010, 0.005\nDN20 2.5: 0.05, 0.025, 0.016, 0.010\nDN20 4: 0.080, 0.040, 0.026, 0.016, 0.008\nDN25 6.3: 0.126, 0.063, 0.040, 0.026\nDN25 10: 0.200, 0.100, 0.064, 0.040\nDN32 6.3: 0.126, 0.063, 0.040, 0.026\nDN32 10: 0.200, 0.100, 0.040\nDN40 10: 0.200, 0.100, 0.064\nDN40 16: 0.320, 0.160, 0.102, 0.064\nDN40 25: 0.500, 0.250, 0.160, 0.100\n\nq4:\nDN15: 2 ×5, 3.125 ×5\nDN20: 3.125 ×4, 5 ×5\nDN25: 7.875 ×4, 12.5 ×4\nDN32: 7.875 ×4, 12.5 ×3\nDN40: 12.5 ×3, 20.0 ×4, 31.25 ×4\n\nratio: 80,160,250,315,400 | 80,160,250,400,800 | 80,160,250,400 | 80,160,250,400,800 | 80,160,250,400 | 80,160,250,400 | 80,160,250,400 | 80,160,400 | 80,160,250 | 80,160,250,400 | 80,160,250,400\n\npressure loss: \nDN15: 0.16×5, 0.25×5\nDN20: 0.16×4, 0.25×5\nDN25: 0.25×4, 0.63×4\nDN32: 0.16×4, 0.25×3\nDN40: 0.16×11\n\nOK. This is a lot of JSON but let's write it.\n\nShould I include models[].attributes? I said designs. For DN15/DN20 models: {\"designs\": [\"A\", \"B\"]}, others {\"designs\": [\"A\"]}. Also maybe software_version/checksum per model? DN15–DN32 (A): SW 1.03 C983; DN40: SW 2.02 CD00; DN15/DN20 B: SW 1.03 F897. I'll include in attributes for completeness: for DN15/DN20 models: {\"designs\": [\"A\",\"B\"], \"software\": {\"A\": \"1.03 (C983)\", \"B\": \"1.03 (F897)\"}}; DN25/DN32: {\"designs\": [\"A\"], \"software\": \"1.03 (C983)\"}; DN40: {\"designs\": [\"A\"], \"software\": \"2.02 (CD00)\"}. This adds value. OK.\n\nType-level characteristics — final list:\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- mechanical_environmental_class? Name: mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- climatic_class: {min:-15,max:70} °C\n- electromagnetic_class: \"E2\"\n- environmental_classification: \"B/O\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- installation_position: \"Horizontal / Vertical\"\n- meter_temperature_class: \"T30, T50, T30/70, T70\"\n- maximum_working_pressure_map: 16, \"bar\"\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6\n- maximum_permitted_error_lower_zone: {min:-5, max:5}, \"%\"\n- scale_interval: 0.001, \"m³\"\n- scale_interval_testing_mode: 0.000001, \"m³\"\n- calculator_capacity: 999999.999, \"m³\"\n- calculator_capacity_testing_mode: 999.999999, \"m³\"\n- battery_chemistry: \"lithium\"\n- battery_voltage: 3.6, \"V\"\n- battery_lifetime: 16, \"years\"\n- integrated_communication: \"Wireless M-Bus, LoRa, NFC, NB-IoT\"\n- microcontroller: \"Renesas RF10WMGAFB MCU (128 KB) or Renesas R5F111PJGFB MCU (256 KB)\"\n\nunit_id: leave null everywhere (I'm not certain of unitsdb IDs; could guess u:degree_Celsius for °C — the instructions allow giving the ID if certain; I'll set null except maybe °C → \"u:degree_Celsius\" which was shown as example. The example in rule 2 shows unit_id \"u:degree_Celsius\" for °C. I can include it. For m³/h etc. I'm less sure; leave null. I'll add \"u:degree_Celsius\" for temperature ones and null elsewhere.)\n\nType_level entries format: each characteristic as a structured value object? The schema: characteristics.type_level: <label>: {value, unit, footnote_markers[]}. The example shape in rule 2 uses unit_symbol/unit_id. I'll use {value, unit_symbol, unit_id} objects.\n\nconfig_level entries format: {attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value, unit_symbol}]}. Include unit_id optionally; keep unit_symbol only.\n\nmodel_level: {attribute, unit_symbol, values: [{model, value}]}.\n\nmatrix_tables: name, columns[], column_units[], rows[], description, footnotes.\n\nLet me also double-check certificate date: \"Date: 28st January 2025\" (signature page). date_issued \"2025-01-28\".\n\nFK-P05-85 — omit (form code). V04/24-12-02 — omit.\n\nNow assemble the JSON. Watch out for the \"Mm\"/\"Bar\" case variations in Tab 5 — normalize to mm/bar.\n\nSoftware matrix rows: software_version strings \"1.03\", \"2.02\". Keep as strings.\n\nWrite model_level arrays carefully. Let me lay out per attribute.\n\nmodels order (index: id):\n1 DN15_Q3=1.6_R80\n2 DN15_Q3=1.6_R160\n3 DN15_Q3=1.6_R250\n4 DN15_Q3=1.6_R315\n5 DN15_Q3=1.6_R400\n6 DN15_Q3=2.5_R80\n7 DN15_Q3=2.